course: politics
lesson: 28
title: The wars the news shows less: Sudan and others
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some wars fill the news every night and others get ten seconds. News teams cover what they can reach safely, what viewers already know, and where big countries are involved, so a war can be huge and still hardly shown.
explain: Sudan is one of these: since April 2023 its army and an armed group called the RSF have fought for power, and the UN reported that more than 12 million people were forced from their homes — imagine every person in a big city walking away at once.
explain: Other long wars and crises include Yemen, the east of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Myanmar and Haiti. Some reach your life in odd ways: a late parcel because ships avoid the Red Sea, or metals in your tablet's battery.
explain: Aid workers run towards danger wearing logos so that fighters know they are there to help, and the rules of war say they must be protected.
explain: Words from the scenes: «y'all» (US South) = you all; «ja» (South Africa) = yes; «sod it» = forget it (rude); «do the needful» (Indian English) = do what is needed, «no?» = isn't it?; «double-double» (Canada) = coffee with two creams and two sugars; «I'm dead, I'm out» = I can't cope, I give up.
fact: A war began in Sudan in April 2023 between the country's army and a powerful armed group called the RSF, news reports said, and each side says the other started it.
fact: The UN reported that more than 12 million people in Sudan were forced from their homes, many fleeing to neighbouring countries such as Chad and Egypt.
fact: In 2024 food experts reported famine in a big camp in Darfur, in western Sudan, and news reports said the city of El Fasher fell to the RSF in late 2025 after a long siege.
fact: Sudan is dangerous and hard for reporters to reach, which is one reason it gets less news time.
fact: The Houthis, an armed group that controls much of Yemen, attacked ships in the Red Sea, saying they acted for Gaza; the US and UK struck back, and many ships went the long way round Africa, news reports said.
fact: Yemen has had a war since 2014 between the Houthis and the government, which was backed by a coalition led by Saudi Arabia; the UN called it one of the world's worst humanitarian crises.
fact: The east of the Democratic Republic of the Congo has had fighting for decades, partly over rich mines whose metals go into electronics.
fact: News reports said the rebel group M23 took the city of Goma in January 2025; UN experts said Rwanda backed M23, which Rwanda denied, and the two countries signed a peace deal in Washington in June 2025.
fact: In 2021 Myanmar's army took power in a coup and a civil war followed; a big earthquake in March 2025 was made harder to deal with by the fighting.
fact: News reports said armed gangs took over much of Port-au-Prince, Haiti's capital, and a police mission led by Kenya came to help.
fact: Reports said 2023 and 2024 were the deadliest years on record for aid workers, and the rules of war say aid workers must be protected.
fact: Researchers who count conflicts reported that the number of wars in the world has risen in recent years.
exercise: Is there a war in Sudan? | Yes, since April 2023, between the army and the RSF | No, Sudan has always been at peace with everyone inside it | No, it is only a football rivalry between two big clubs | Only in old history books, about the wars from long ago
exercise: Who are the two main sides in Sudan's war? | The army and an armed group called the RSF | Sudan and Australia | Two big football clubs | The UN and NATO
exercise: About how many people in Sudan were forced from their homes, the UN reported? | More than 12 million | About 12 | About 1,200 | None at all
exercise: Where did many people fleeing Sudan go? | Neighbouring countries like Chad and Egypt | New Zealand by boat | Antarctica | Nowhere, because nobody left
exercise: What does «famine» mean? | Food running out so badly that many people starve | A kind of fancy party food | A long rainy season | A festival with lots of food
exercise: Sam thought famines were only in history books. What was reported in 2024? | Food experts reported famine in a camp in Darfur | The last famine in the world ended in the Middle Ages | Famines became impossible everywhere after the year 1900 | Famine was reported only in Europe, after a very bad harvest
exercise: Why does Sudan get less news than Ukraine or Gaza? | It is dangerous for reporters, and fewer viewers know about it | Its people matter less | Nothing is happening there now, because the war ended quietly a long time ago | It has no television at all
exercise: Why did Liam's uncle's parcel arrive late? | Ships avoided the Red Sea after attacks and went round Africa | Parcels are banned from Africa by a new law, so every parcel was opened and checked by hand | The post office was on holiday | Ships can only sail on Mondays
exercise: Who attacked ships in the Red Sea? | The Houthis, an armed group in Yemen | Pirates from a cartoon | Australia's navy | The Red Cross
exercise: How long has Yemen had a war? | Since 2014 | Since last week | Since the year 1066 | It has never had one
exercise: How is Noah's tablet linked to the DR Congo? | Metals in its battery may come from mines in eastern Congo | Tablets are only made in Congo's schools, by pupils, as part of their lessons each afternoon | Congo invented the tablet | The tablet's screen shows a map of Congo
exercise: What did M23 do in January 2025, as reported? | Took the city of Goma | Won the football World Cup | Signed a peace deal in Paris | Left Africa entirely for good
exercise: Why was earthquake help hard to get into Myanmar? | A civil war has gone on since the army took power in 2021 | Myanmar has no roads at all, only rivers and narrow mountain paths | Earthquakes never need any help from other countries at all | The sea froze all around it, so no ships could get in or out
exercise: Why can't some children in Haiti go to school? | Gangs control many streets of the capital | Haiti banned all schools | It is always the summer holidays | All the teachers moved to Kenya
exercise: Why do aid workers wear a logo? | So fighters know they are there to help | Because it is fashionable | To advertise a shop | To show which side they fight for
exercise: Ethan says «I can't do anything anyway.» What is true? | You can't fix every war, but learning, kindness and giving all count | Nothing anyone does ever helps | Children must stop every war alone | Wars are getting fewer every year, so soon there will be nothing left to do anyway
test: Jake in Florida says: «y'all, ten seconds on Sudan and then football, so it's no big deal, right?» How do you correct it? | It is one of the world's biggest crises; little news time doesn't mean small | Right, little news time means a small war, because the news always shows the biggest ones longest | Sudan's war ended in 2023 | It is a football rivalry, not a war
test: Sam's friend from Durban says: «ja, but famine is just old history, hey.» How do you correct him? | Famine was reported in Darfur in 2024; it happens when wars block food | Famine stopped forever in 1900, when farmers learnt to grow enough food for everyone | Famine only happens in winter | Famine means eating too many sweets
test: Someone mutters: «why should I care, Sudan's miles away, sod it.» What is the fair reply? | Millions of people like you lost their homes; caring and learning still count | It doesn't affect any real people, because only soldiers live in the places where the fighting is | Sudan is closer than your street | Caring about it is against the rules
test: What would happen to deliveries if ships kept avoiding the Red Sea? | Goods would take longer, because ships go round Africa | Parcels would arrive faster | Ships would sail over Egypt | Nothing would change
test: Priya's auntie says: «do the needful and give to Yemen also.» Priya asks: «but the war is in Gaza, no?» What is the best answer? | Yemen has had its own long war since 2014, one of the worst crises | Yemen is a city inside Gaza | Yemen has no war at all, so the charity is collecting money for a country that is fine | The charity got the country's name wrong
test: A strategy game shows eastern Congo as nothing but mines, with no people. What is the real picture? | Millions live there, and fighting over mines has hurt families for decades | Eastern Congo is an empty desert, so the game is right that only the mines matter there | The mines belong to Antarctica | Congo has no metals at all
test: Chloe's dad in Ottawa, holding his double-double, asks: «why can't they just fly help into Myanmar?» What made help hard? | The civil war since the 2021 coup made getting aid in difficult | Myanmar has no airports at all, so planes have nowhere to land in the whole country | Earthquakes never need help | Nobody lives in Myanmar
test: Tyler in Miami says: «Haiti's war is two armies, right?» How do you correct it? | It is mostly armed gangs controlling streets, with foreign police sent to help | Two armies from Europe fight there over the island, and Haiti's own people have left | There is no trouble in Haiti | Haiti is at war with Kenya
test: Fatima asks her aunt: «Won't they shoot you?» Which answer is true? | The rules of war protect aid workers, but 2023 and 2024 were the deadliest years for them | Aid workers are never in any danger, because every fighter in every war always obeys the rules | Aid workers carry the biggest guns | The logo makes bullets bounce off
test: Ethan in Calgary groans: «more wars than almost any time since World War Two? I'm dead, I'm out.» What is a helpful reply? | It's normal to feel overwhelmed; you can still learn, be kind and help a charity | Hide from every news story forever | It's fake, because wars are disappearing all over the world and there are fewer now than ever before | Only adults are allowed to care
test: Which sentence about Rwanda and M23 is fair? | UN experts said Rwanda backed M23; Rwanda denied it and said it was protecting its border | Everyone agrees Rwanda had nothing to do with it, including the UN experts who went and looked | Rwanda said it leads M23 itself | M23 is a football club
exam: Identify the parties to the war which commenced in Sudan in April 2023. | The armed forces of Sudan and the paramilitary Rapid Support Forces | The Republic of Sudan and the Commonwealth of Australia | The United Nations and the North Atlantic Treaty Organization, acting jointly against Egypt | Two rival national football associations
exam: State the number of persons reported by the United Nations to have been displaced from their homes in Sudan. | In excess of twelve million | Approximately twelve hundred | Approximately twelve persons in all | None whatsoever in any region
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the principal reason for the comparatively scant coverage of Sudan? | The danger to, and difficulty of access for, reporters, combined with lesser familiarity among viewers | The absence of any conflict whatsoever in Sudan since the conclusion of a peace agreement in the year 2023 | A prohibition upon any mention of Sudan in all broadcasts | The lesser importance of the lives of its inhabitants
exam: Select the response consistent with the established reports concerning shipping in the Red Sea. | Following attacks by the Houthis, many vessels were diverted around the African continent | Vessels were conveyed overland across Egypt upon specially constructed railways, avoiding the sea entirely | All shipping throughout the world ceased permanently | The Red Sea was drained so as to permit passage
